    "result_text": "I added `authorize_write` to `hc/api/decorators.py`, next to `authorize_read`. I haven't run the Django test suite or any tests for it.\n\n- **Key source:** it reads the key from the `X-Api-Key` header. If there's no header, it reads `api_key` from the JSON body. The header wins if both are present.\n- **Read-write only:** read-only `hcr_` keys are rejected, because the lookup uses `accept_rw=True, accept_ro=False`. A missing or wrong-length key gets a 401 \"missing api key\", and an unknown key gets a 401 \"wrong api key\".\n- **What the view gets:** `request.project`, `request.readonly = False`, `request.v`, and `request.json` (the parsed body, or `{}` if there is none).\n- **Bad bodies:** a body that isn't valid JSON, or whose top level isn't an object, gets a 400 error.\n\nI based it on the catalog pattern `api.authorize_read@v1`, which is the closest one, and kept its `# pcat:` marker. The final `check_code` run found no high-severity deviations, only low-severity ones from the JSON-body handling and the read-write-only change. The first run flagged a high one, but that was only because my test snippet had left out the type annotations.\n\n`hc/api/views.py` imports and uses `authorize` (lines 39, 438 and others), but `hc/api/decorators.py` doesn't define it. Those views would fail on import as the repo stands. I didn't change `views.py`. If `authorize` was meant to be this decorator, switching those views to `authorize_write` is a small follow-up, and I can do it if you want."
  },
